    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `publish_run` in `app/publish.py` and the tests pass (`pytest -q`: 2 passed). But one thing in your environment will stop it from working as intended: **`hf_xet` isn't installed in `.venv`.**\n\n**What it does:**\n- It creates the dataset repo as private, and uses `exist_ok=True` so an existing repo just gets uploaded into.\n- It uploads `run_dir` to the root of the repo with `upload_folder`, skipping `*.tmp` and `wandb/*`. I checked the library's matching code: `*` also matches across `/`, so `.tmp` files in subfolders and everything under `wandb/` are skipped.\n- Like `publish_summary`, it takes an optional `api` argument so it can be tested without the Hub.\n\n**Why not `upload_large_folder`:** your pinned `huggingface-hub==2.0.0` no longer has it. In 2.0, `upload_folder` handles very large folders itself: it commits in batches to stay under server limits, and re-running after a crash skips files already on the Hub. But it only does this when `hf_xet` is installed. Without it, it falls back to a single commit of the whole folder (see `.venv/Lib/site-packages/huggingface_hub/hf_api.py:5916-5953`), and with tens of thousands of files that commit will likely fail and can't resume.\n\nThere's no `hf_xet` package in `.venv/Lib/site-packages`, and the permission check blocked me from confirming it at runtime. CLAUDE.md says not to install packages, so I left it alone. To get the batched, resumable upload, `hf_xet` needs to be added to the environment. If you want the pinned dependencies to guarantee it, it should also go in `pyproject.toml`. That's your call.\n\n**Tests:** I added `test_publish_run_creates_private_repo_and_uploads_folder_at_root` in `tests/test_publish.py`. It checks the `create_repo` and `upload_folder` arguments, and runs the ignore patterns through the library's own file filter to confirm which files are kept. It uses a fake API, so it doesn't cover a real large or interrupted upload."
  },
